T:What it is
T:A number is
T:a
T:mathematical
T:object used
T:to count,
T:measure, and
T:label. The
T:most basic
T:examples are
T:the natural
T:numbers: 1,
T:2, 3, 4, 5,
T:and so
T:forth.
T:Individual
T:numbers can
T:be
T:represented
T:in spoken or
T:written
T:language
T:with number
T:words, or
T:with
T:dedicated
T:symbols
T:called
T:numerals;
T:for example,
T:"eleven" is
T:a number
T:word and
T:"11" is the
T:correspondin
T:numeral. As
T:only a
T:limited list
T:of symbols
T:can be
T:memorized, a
T:numeral
T:system is
T:used to
T:represent
T:any number
T:in an
T:organized
T:way. The
T:most common
T:representati
T:is the
T:Hindu–Arabic
T:numeral
T:system, a
T:decimal
T:system which
T:can display
T:any
T:non-negative
T:integer
T:using a
T:combination
T:of ten
T:Arabic
T:numeral
T:symbols
T:called
T:digits.
T:Numerals can
T:be used for
T:counting,
T:for
T:labelling,
T:for
T:ordering,
T:and for
T:codes. In
T:common
T:usage,
T:however, a
T:numeral is
T:not clearly
T:distinguishe
T:from the
T:number that
T:it
T:represents.
